 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training is accelerated by data parallelism [S1]. MegaNeRF improves training speed by 3x compared to baseline approaches [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** By partitioning the scene, each submodule only needs to store parameters for its local region, reducing memory requirements per GPU [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Achieves a 40x speedup over conventional NeRF rendering while remaining within 0.8 dB in PSNR quality [S1]. On existing datasets (Quad 6k and UrbanScene3D), it improves PSNR by 12% [S1].
-*   **Scaling:** Decouples rendering time from scene size, allowing rendering to scale to arbitrarily large environments [S6].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables interactive fly-throughs for large-scale urban scenes (150,000 to over 1,300,000 m²) [S1]. Handles varying lighting with per-image appearance embeddings [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a ground-level parameter to prevent sampling below ground [S3]. Blending at module boundaries can be complex [S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training is parallelized across $N$ submodules, reducing the training time per GPU by a factor of approximately 10x due to data partitioning [S1]. Overall training speed is improved by 3x compared to baseline approaches on existing datasets [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Each submodule stores a smaller MLP, reducing memory requirements per GPU. However, the total memory across all GPUs is higher than a single global NeRF. The octree cache (if used) adds memory overhead proportional to the scene size [S2].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Achieves a 40x speedup over conventional NeRF rendering while remaining within 0.8 dB in PSNR quality [S1, S6]. Improves PSNR by 12% compared to baseline approaches on existing datasets [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Scales to scenes 1000x larger than prior work (e.g., 150,000 to over 1,300,000 $m^2$) [S1]. Rendering time is decoupled from scene size, allowing rendering to scale to arbitrarily large environments [S6].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables training on massive datasets that are infeasible for a single NeRF. Supports interactive fly-throughs. Handles varying lighting via appearance embeddings [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the ground-level parameter to avoid underground sampling [S3]. Boundary artifacts may occur if blending is not handled correctly [S3].
 
 ## Background
-MegaNeRF was introduced in 2022 [S1]. It was motivated by the need for search-and-rescue applications where drones provide inexpensive means of quickly surveying large areas [S1]. The method builds on NeRF++ by using an ellipsoid for the foreground to achieve tighter bounds on the region of interest and adding altitude constraints to skip underground sampling [S1]. It extends NeRF++'s inverse sphere parameterization to ellipsoids for aerial scenes [S2].
+MegaNeRF was introduced in 2021/2022 by Xiangli et al. [S1, S6]. It was motivated by the need for search-and-rescue applications where drones provide inexpensive means of quickly surveying large areas [S1]. The method builds on NeRF++ [S1, S2] by replacing the unit sphere foreground model with an ellipsoid and adding altitude constraints. It also incorporates appearance embeddings from NeRF-W [S2]. The core insight is that visibility statistics for large-scale scenes show that only a small fraction of training images are visible from any particular scene point, motivating a sparse network structure with specialized parameters for different regions [S1].
 
